#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166465 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74b8d3a6-0298-5f65-ad28-e2fc3cde731d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent to a Trajectory Given an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "86054cff-8fae-561d-a57e-31e573a45a91" :question/math-academy-id "e-21585" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/assets/d8be54eddd0358742170a03b3c55e0cb94eaf2a7d59f12e1c4361a6197d29847.png)\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\ln 2?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\ln 2$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & ={e}^{3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+2{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =8[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+2\\cdot 2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =8[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+4[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2(12)+1(4) \\\\ 1(12)+2(4)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}24+4 \\\\ 12+8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}28 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166466 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d219270-5202-54e6-8cba-dc816155bd7b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Nullclines of a System of Differential Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "63995ae4-21fa-5777-85ca-93585258b9f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-21586" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Find equations of the $x$- and $y$-nullclines for the corresponding phase space." :question/worked-solution "Recall that: \n\n- The $x$-nullcline is a line for which $x' = 0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are vertical). So, to find it, we set the first equation of the system equal to $0{:}$\n\n$$\n3x-y=0 \n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\ny = 3x\n$$\n\n- The $y$-nullcline is a line for which $y' = 0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are horizontal). So, to find it, we set the second equation of the system equal to $0{:}$\n\n$$\n-2x-y=0 \n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\ny = -2x\n$$\n\nPlotting these in the phase space, we obtain the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/assets/66cdd05ac68d762c5b78df40b22f9e0cd660099c13e48e9eb71c81c73a044280.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166467 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "129bee33-7c18-5450-affe-dd710a3a75dd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Trajectory Given a Starting Point and the Vector Field"}] :question/id #uuid "e0888bda-120f-52bb-a6f9-c5af9737139f" :question/math-academy-id "e-21588" :question/problem "A sketch of the phase space with nullclines and the corresponding direction field of tangents to the solutions of a system of differential equations is shown below (the tangents are not drawn to scale). Sketch the trajectory of the solution starting at the marked point.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/assets/0cf7942e618f0465ead5b98c6404a3747e505db75517725971c833c97121ab64.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the trajectory.\n\n- We start at the given point, moving along with the flow (downward and slightly to the right) until the trajectory intersects the $x$-nullcline, where the tangents are vertical.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/assets/105bdbdcac5f0ff20f71a0131bf5bb3cbafe0e93ce8bc733a51c2dcb3fb8364f.png)\n\n- Next, the flow continues downward, but the trajectory starts to turn slightly to the left.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/assets/96757a3aee3b161b2ba9887cd85f977f063807779604647dd791cf265790cabc.png)\n\n- After that, the trajectory continues to go downwards and slightly to the left to infinity.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/assets/658c15d2291368d0e6836ba19b90157d49dee4a510d58cddf7f85ddc9ff72a8d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356544 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74b8d3a6-0298-5f65-ad28-e2fc3cde731d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent to a Trajectory Given an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dd17ac0-f812-5fc8-a169-5f187a942545" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356546} {:answer/id #uuid "98ffdb4e-121c-5e5e-ab54-3194ebb64839" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356547} {:answer/id #uuid "df2c57e2-7db9-5c0b-b79b-190ecbb971b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356548} {:answer/id #uuid "3b8787fa-b703-56c6-8c95-50dea1c67e9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356549} {:answer/id #uuid "e2ff297a-fa5e-52f2-83c3-895b460968a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df2c57e2-7db9-5c0b-b79b-190ecbb971b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356548} :answer-field/id #uuid "332208d8-cbb8-5439-8cd9-3d7092749f63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6c25cae4-5e98-5fb9-80f0-9786e8d8a695" :question/math-academy-id "q-332607" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above and its trajectory passing through the point $(-1,2)$ in the phase space of the system. What is the tangent vector to the trajectory at this point?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nIn our case, the trajectory (solution) passes through the point $(x,y)=(-1,2)$ in the phase space. Substituting the coordinates of this point into the equations of our system, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =-3x+y \\\\  & =-3(-1)+2 \\\\  & =3+2 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =2x+4y \\\\  & =2(-1)+4(2) \\\\  & =-2+8 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent vector to the trajectory is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356551 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74b8d3a6-0298-5f65-ad28-e2fc3cde731d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent to a Trajectory Given an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d81bc0e4-ffac-50bf-b538-22ce65da5230" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356553} {:answer/id #uuid "ecbb6ede-1585-560e-855a-5223ec215d8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356554} {:answer/id #uuid "9708421d-745e-5d9e-b394-beb3b271025a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356555} {:answer/id #uuid "4b176b4c-bf2d-5108-be11-b6c695c84be5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356556} {:answer/id #uuid "4bc57c5b-4ac1-52c7-af25-ca17f988dec8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bc57c5b-4ac1-52c7-af25-ca17f988dec8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356557} :answer-field/id #uuid "54f5e225-d732-5dd7-b910-3aefc1c03bab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356552}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0edb7bdb-b6a4-50c3-b4ac-463fffe106e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-332612" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above and its trajectory passing through the point $(-1,-2)$ in the phase space of the system. What is the tangent vector to the trajectory at this point?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nIn our case, the trajectory (solution) passes through the point $(x,y)=(-1,-2)$ in the phase space. Substituting the coordinates of this point into the equations of our system, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =2x+y \\\\  & =2(-1)+(-2) \\\\  & =-2-2 \\\\  & =-4 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-x+3y \\\\  & =-(-1)+3(-2) \\\\  & =1-6 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent vector to the trajectory is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74b8d3a6-0298-5f65-ad28-e2fc3cde731d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent to a Trajectory Given an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c6dfaea-b072-53f7-9c5c-d8624acbdc18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356560} {:answer/id #uuid "ebb4692f-4c8d-58a2-be12-d8fbacbc2773" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356561} {:answer/id #uuid "a5adf7ad-5e77-57b7-8e35-f06ce56576cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356562} {:answer/id #uuid "0f765c89-7221-5f96-ba05-085952c1bd9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356563} {:answer/id #uuid "bc54e4e3-3336-5f61-8c25-2c83020e4a91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f765c89-7221-5f96-ba05-085952c1bd9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356563} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e04ea8d-6e65-5782-b4f2-ff8e54b410df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c316bd8b-521b-5177-a038-0d7ba4ee45c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-332614" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above and its trajectory passing through the point $(0,-2)$ in the phase space of the system. What is the tangent vector to the trajectory at this point?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nIn our case, the trajectory (solution) passes through the point $(x,y)=(0,-2)$ in the phase space. Substituting the coordinates of this point into the equations of our system, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =3x-2y \\\\  & =3(0)-2(-2) \\\\  & =0+4 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-4x+y \\\\  & =-4(0)+(-2) \\\\  & =0-2 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent vector to the trajectory is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356565 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74b8d3a6-0298-5f65-ad28-e2fc3cde731d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent to a Trajectory Given an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "379038b0-c445-55e4-982d-ec8ebd3ad714" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 48\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356567} {:answer/id #uuid "84bb893d-40e1-530e-9409-ec378e47918d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 48\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356568} {:answer/id #uuid "aa251ca4-4b76-5ee3-9e5a-ff5a435aa9e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356569} {:answer/id #uuid "1bba3760-918b-5641-a3cd-bac39c0b9ba9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 192\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356570} {:answer/id #uuid "6f4d3749-8bf9-5af8-94c9-2ba0bdd462bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}192 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bba3760-918b-5641-a3cd-bac39c0b9ba9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 192\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356570} :answer-field/id #uuid "96beea68-0cd0-553a-865f-53377d322948" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "28e64722-99a7-5d4a-b749-394a97d4ce0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-332616" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,x(t)={e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & ={e}^{2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+16[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 48\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 48\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 48\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2(4)+0(48) \\\\ 0(4)+4(48)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 192\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74b8d3a6-0298-5f65-ad28-e2fc3cde731d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent to a Trajectory Given an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c2da436-5360-5224-ab1a-e7dd9c191fbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356574} {:answer/id #uuid "47aba498-4682-51ef-9b61-35ac07e68528" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356575} {:answer/id #uuid "85c3be5b-21fd-517b-affb-d10134bf7ae5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356576} {:answer/id #uuid "90bf1c3e-98ac-57e9-88aa-466e7cf103f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356577} {:answer/id #uuid "f4ecd2da-b7e6-55e2-8a01-00711bceada7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47aba498-4682-51ef-9b61-35ac07e68528" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356575} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9564b08-a323-5b7e-a1f5-fa1e25b6876a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "be9877ee-3abb-5c56-bc3e-693bac7c1ef7" :question/math-academy-id "q-332619" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,x(t)={e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & ={e}^{-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2(\\frac{1}{3})+0(6) \\\\ 0(\\frac{1}{3})+1(6)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356579 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d219270-5202-54e6-8cba-dc816155bd7b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Nullclines of a System of Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4e0432c-6118-5d74-b53b-14ade4a5738e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356581} {:answer/id #uuid "9d4d2692-ef9b-571c-9080-8ad3e88540d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356582} {:answer/id #uuid "da01f43b-0a1a-5b4f-a31f-2dfafca3488f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356583} {:answer/id #uuid "026fbc42-f996-51e5-a8ee-518574a08856" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da01f43b-0a1a-5b4f-a31f-2dfafca3488f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356583} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7f9ba7d-b3e9-55a5-a803-74354ba173b9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356580} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f7b34f5-6b63-58b2-8dcb-a075b79d327c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356586} {:answer/id #uuid "394db3f5-1d9a-57b9-9cff-d09ed7f2d953" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math