\n- The $y$-nullcline is a line for which ${y}^{′}=0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are horizontal). So, to find it, we set the second equation of the system equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n-x-3y=0\\,⇒\\,y=-\\frac{1}{3}x\n$$\n\nPlotting these in the phase space, we obtain the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/assets/a9c0fb2365806ddb02ff8c60a618aabf4cc825917c29fb472ee3bab2492b9033.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356623 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d219270-5202-54e6-8cba-dc816155bd7b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Nullclines of a System of Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fad33e6d-f667-5ca1-89a0-8f877eb87e5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356625} {:answer/id #uuid "ec7d40d6-b95f-5bbe-9b05-0ee36430042d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356626} {:answer/id #uuid "dc12559c-4e47-5703-b818-321a94874efc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356627} {:answer/id #uuid "0ca8fde5-204d-5c47-884f-e2a5f0ea4e14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc12559c-4e47-5703-b818-321a94874efc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356627} :answer-field/id #uuid "da7ad89b-158e-5e3b-b582-31350580a911" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356624} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95f3fdb4-9aee-5df9-ab26-e396cde0804b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356630} {:answer/id #uuid "7d6f9fbf-15b9-55b5-b49e-8692cb728560" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356631} {:answer/id #uuid "5c737e51-7e2a-5d13-bf95-50a262201103" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356632} {:answer/id #uuid "4690b7d1-4459-58d4-8768-e42eae6a7965" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c737e51-7e2a-5d13-bf95-50a262201103" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356632} :answer-field/id #uuid "139d91c7-7a4d-5715-b4dc-decff6391391" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356629}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ad06d77e-d045-5115-beff-bb5f67391033" :question/math-academy-id "q-332662" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Find equations of the $x$- and $y$-nullclines for the corresponding phase space.\n$\\,$\nThe $x$-nullcline is $y=$ {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-nullcline is $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall that: \n\n- The $x$-nullcline is a line for which ${x}^{′}=0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are vertical). So, to find it, we set the first equation of the system equal to $0:$\n\n$$\nx+y=0\\,⇒\\,y=-x\n$$\n\n- The $y$-nullcline is a line for which ${y}^{′}=0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are horizontal). So, to find it, we set the second equation of the system equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n-4x+y=0\\,⇒\\,y=4x\n$$\n\nPlotting these in the phase space, we obtain the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/assets/e1d157e7c921aa6fd2dd5449934baf0258dc5a17fc9f6e6db672a783d59553ca.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356634 :knowledge-point/_question